-- title:
--   Existence of a dimension for a p-divisible group
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative noetherian local ring which is complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, let $p$ be a prime number with $p \in \mathfrak m_R$, let $h$ be a natural number, and let $G$ be a $p$-divisible group of height $h$ over $R$ in the sense of the structure [`PDivisibleGroup`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199): a family of commutative $R$-algebras `G.level v`, each a cocommutative Hopf algebra over $R$ that is finite and free as an $R$-module of rank $p^{v h}$, together with surjective coalgebra-and-algebra maps $\mathrm{transition}_v : \mathrm{level}(v+1) \to \mathrm{level}(v)$ whose kernel is the image of the augmentation ideal of $\mathrm{level}(v+1)$ under multiplication by $p^{v}$ on that Hopf algebra. The assertion is that there exists a natural number $n$ such that $G$ `HasDimension` $n$, that is, for every $v$ the cotangent module $I_v/I_v^2$ of the augmentation ideal $I_v$ of `G.level v` admits an $R$-linear isomorphism onto $(R/(p^v))^n$, where $(p^v)$ is the ideal generated by $p^v$. Only the existence of such isomorphisms, level by level, is claimed; no compatibility between the levels is asserted.
--
--   This is the statement that a $p$-divisible group over a complete noetherian local ring of residue characteristic $p$ has a well-defined dimension, the cotangent spaces of its levels being free of constant rank over $R/p^vR$ (Tate's Proposition 1 in the connected case). It supplies the dimension used in the subsequent analysis of Tate modules of $p$-divisible groups over rings of integers, in particular in the results on bases diagonalising the cyclotomic character and on groups with trivial inertia action.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_PDivisibleGroup_exists_hasDimension.lean

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_PDivisibleGroup_Dimension

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
